COMMENT  UP.CLI

COMMENT  Check if the two arguments are equal.  If so, the macro is executable.
[!equal,1,2]

  COMMENT  Check to see if the process is PID 2.
  [!ueq,2,[!pid]]

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Start SYSLOG and set system ID, SYSID, here.

    COMMENT  Set search list, dir to the root directory, push an
    COMMENT  environment level, and turn Superuser on.
    searchlist  :util  :
    directory   :
    push
    prompt      pop
    superuser   on

    COMMENT  Set up and start a CLI log file to help track UP errors.
    write
    write    Starting log file UP.LOG. Type this file to track any error.
    delete/2=ignore       :UP.LOG
    create/elementsize=8  :UP.LOG
    logfile               :UP.LOG

    COMMENT  Set tape unit ACLs.
    acl      @MT-  +,ware
    COMMENT  to system manager:  Set diskette unit ACLs to +,WARE here.

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Set default characteristics of console
    COMMENT                      lines as needed here, via
    COMMENT                      CHARACTERISTICS/DEFAULT  @CONn  commands.

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Initialize other LDUs, if you have them, here.
    COMMENT  to system manager:  Set ACLs on newly initialized LDUs, if desired.

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Initialize all job processors, if more than one
    COMMENT                    by removing 'COMMENT' for EACH JP in the system:
    COMMENT          JPINITIALIZE/2=WARNING 0
    COMMENT          JPINITIALIZE/2=WARNING 1
    COMMENT          JPINITIALIZE/2=WARNING 2
    COMMENT          JPINITIALIZE/2=WARNING 3


    COMMENT  Run QCMP to delete unofficial files from queue directory.
    write    Running QCMP to delete unofficial files from directory :QUEUE.
    xeq/1=warning/2=warning      QCMP/yes

    COMMENT  Create EXEC process.
    write
    write    Starting EXEC.
    process/default/directory=@/name=EXEC  EXEC
    WAIT_FOR_PORT  @EXEC

    COMMENT  Tell EXEC to start batch output queues.
    COMMENT  to system manager:  Correct the string for your printer, if needed.

    string   @LPB

    write
    write    Starting batch output queues.
    control  @EXEC  start  BATCH_OUTPUT  [!string]
    control  @EXEC  start  BATCH_LIST    [!string]

    COMMENT  Start default print queue and continue printer.
    write
    write    Starting default print queue and continuing printer.
    control  @EXEC  start  LPT           [!string]
    control  @EXEC  continue             [!string]

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Tell EXEC to start any other printers here.

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Start any network software here.
    COMMENT  to system manager:  Start any local data management software,
    COMMENT                      like INFOS II, DG/DBMS, or DG/SQL here.
    COMMENT  to system manager:  Start CEO, if present, here.

    COMMENT  to system manager:  Start your own applications, if any, here.

    COMMENT  Tell EXEC to open the mount queue.
    control  @EXEC  open  MOUNTQ

    COMMENT  Tell EXEC to continue batch stream 1.
    COMMENT  to system manager:  You can set batch stream qpriorities here and
    COMMENT                      start streams other than 1 here, if you want.
    write
    write    Continuing batch stream.
    control  @EXEC  continue  1

    COMMENT  Tell EXEC to enable all consoles.
    write
    control  @EXEC  enable/all

    COMMENT  Return to original environment.  Set up informative prompt.
    checkterms
    pop
    prompt   time   checkterms

    COMMENT  to system manager:  For a secure system console, execute :LOCK_CLI
    COMMENT                      next, instead of :CLI.

    execute  :CLI

  [!else]
    write  *Error*
    write  %0\% runs only from the master CLI -- PID 2.,,Your PID is [!pid].
    write  Required arguments:,,None
    write  Optional switches:,,,None
  [!end]

[!else]
  write    This macro is executable only if -- in its first !EQUAL command --
  write    the numbers are the same.  To make the macro executable
  write    use a text editor to make the numbers equal. For example
  write    make them both 1.
  write
  write    We suggest that you keep the executable macro in the root
  write    directory. This will provide easy access on startup.
  write    You can move it there via the command ,, MOVE/V/R : UP.CLI
[!end]
